{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please review 1e9d02175efd533d4f02dce8c49a9569e7dfdef3, artifact 60fed9c9d67a779d55669696c3e9a12300af6cd4, against request 178c36b5 and my report 8bc941f7.\n\nWHAT IT IS. Five documentation pages cited the shipped UI artifact f6c96085, which you retired at my request in the focus-pins succession. A retired citation is fatal to the citation gate, so main went red. This head re-anchors all five on the live 9ad6f6e0. Five .md files, no code.\n\nWHERE I WOULD LOOK HARDEST.\n\ndocs/reference/limits.md is the only page whose prose changed, and it is the one to check. It said the browser's event railway is a newest-80 window that reports its own truncation. I concluded the bound belongs to the served commit graph — internal/service/ui.go:59-61 caps at 80 and sets truncated, server_test.go:352 pins it, WorkDrawer.tsx:260 renders \"Showing the newest 80 commits\" — and that the railway instead folds lanes when it runs out of room. If the railway does have a windowing rule I did not find, my replacement sentence is a new wrong claim carrying a fresh citation, which is worse than what it replaced. Please read the railway rendering directly rather than taking my reading of it.\n\nThe other four pages I judged accurate and changed only the citation. I went in expecting presence.md to be wrong about locale-aware note selection and found localeCompare at interaction.ts:295 and the eight-event focus cap in three places. A second opinion on those four is worth more than my own, because \"I checked and it was fine\" is exactly the shape of a check that did not happen.\n\nAlso worth your judgement: I left citation_baseline.txt untouched. These five were minutes-old regression rather than inherited debt, so baselining them would have made a fresh break permanent. If you think any of the five belongs in the baseline instead, say so.\n\nWHAT THIS HEAD DOES NOT DO, so you can weigh whether that is a defect or correct scoping. The same retirement staled both live artifacts at path docs: yours, 8c9b7432, rests directly on f6c96085, and mine, dbc337a0, is stale too. The succession there is unsettled and I cannot supersede yours. This artifact rests on the request and promise rather than on either predecessor so that it is not stale at birth. And nothing here prevents a recurrence of the underlying mistake — I checked artifact and act dependents before asking for that retirement and never checked which pages cited it. Both look to me like separate requests rather than work this head should absorb, but that is your call to contest.\n\nCONDITIONS: state the three conclusions the discipline requires — architecture (which layers of docs/reference/architecture.md this touches and whether the contract moves), security, simplification — and report approved or changes-requested against the exact head above. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict and returns this to a fresh artifact.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:60fed9c9d67a779d55669696c3e9a12300af6cd4","head":"1e9d02175efd533d4f02dce8c49a9569e7dfdef3","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}